NASA has established significant use of AM for heat exchangers, such as liquid rocket engine combustion chambers and channel-cooled nozzles. Various applications have been reported including combustion chambers using copper-alloys such as GRCop-42, GRCop-42, C18150, and also Inconel 718 and Bimetallic Copper-Superalloy structures that have accumulated over 30,000 s and 400 starts of hot-fire time. Channel-cooled nozzles in various alloys including JBK-75, NASA HR-1, Inconel 625, Haynes 230, and bimetallic (Copper-Superalloy) structures have also been demonstrated by NASA and accumulated over 11,000 s and 250+ hot-fire tests [133]. While these combustion chambers and nozzles meet performance requirements, they also demonstrated significant cost and schedule savings for hardware delivery [134]. An example of combustion chambers and nozzles for liquid rocket engines can be seen in Fig. 5 [135], [136], [137].
